# QODIQA Production Environment Template
#
# Copy this file to .env.prod (or load via your secret manager) and replace
# every <...> placeholder with a real value before deploying.
#
# SECURITY:
#   • Never commit a file with real values to version control.
#   • This template file is safe to commit — it contains NO real secrets.
#   • All <...> placeholders are deliberately invalid and will cause startup
#     to fail with ENV_VALIDATION_FATAL or SECRET_VALIDATION_FATAL, preventing
#     accidental deployment with placeholder values.
#   • In production, prefer injecting these values via a secret management
#     service (AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, HashiCorp Vault, GCP
#     Secret Manager) rather than a plain .env file on disk.
#
# VALIDATION:
#   QODIQA validates all variables at startup.
#   Missing required variables → process.exit(1) with a FATAL log event.
#   Placeholder values in production → process.exit(1) with SECRET_VALIDATION_FATAL.
#   Invalid formats or out-of-range values → process.exit(1) with ENV_VALIDATION_FATAL.
#
# GENERATION COMMANDS:
#   KEK:          openssl rand -hex 32
#   Token/Secret: openssl rand -hex 32
#   Password:     openssl rand -base64 32
#

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# APPLICATION-CONFIG SECRETS PROVIDER
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Purpose:    Everything marked "Security: High/Critical" below (Redis/Postgres
#             passwords, AI provider API keys, QODIQA_OPS_TOKEN, alerting/log-
#             shipping credentials) can be sourced from HashiCorp Vault KV v2
#             instead of a plaintext value in this file. This is SEPARATE from
#             QODIQA_KMS_PROVIDER above — that selects the backend for the KEK
#             specifically; this selects the backend for every OTHER secret.
#             See SECRETS_MANAGEMENT.md's "Application-config secrets provider
#" section for the full,
#             authoritative list of which variables this covers
#             (src/infra/configSecrets/managedSecrets.js is the source of truth).
# Required:   NO (default: "env" — read from this file / the process
#             environment, exactly as).
# Values:     env | vault-kv
# Security:   When set to a non-"env" value, every managed variable below MUST
#             be OMITTED from this file (or any other .env loaded in
#             production) — the provider becomes the EXCLUSIVE source for
#             those names; a value still present here is deliberately cleared
#             from process.env at startup, never silently used as a fallback.
#             If the provider cannot be reached, startup FAILS CLOSED
#             (process.exit(1)) rather than falling back to this file.
#
# QODIQA_CONFIG_SECRETS_PROVIDER=vault-kv
# QODIQA_CONFIG_SECRETS_VAULT_MOUNT=secret
# QODIQA_CONFIG_SECRETS_VAULT_PATH=qodiqa/app-config
#
# Auth for the vault-kv config-secrets backend reuses the SAME VAULT_ADDR /
# VAULT_ROLE_ID / VAULT_SECRET_ID / VAULT_TOKEN / VAULT_NAMESPACE /
# VAULT_TLS_CA_CERT variables the KMS vault-kv/vault-transit provider uses
# (see "KMS/Vault backend configuration" further below) — one Vault
# deployment, a second KV v2 path. These must be present in the REAL process
# environment (shell export, Kubernetes Secret env, etc.), never only in this
# file — the config-secrets bootstrap runs before dotenv loads this file.

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REQUIRED SECRETS
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Key Encryption Key ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    AES-256-GCM encryption key for the Ed25519 signing key store.
# Required:   YES
# Format:     Exactly 64 hexadecimal characters (256 bits / 32 bytes).
# Security:   Critical — loss or exposure requires full key rotation.
#             Never store on disk in plaintext in production.
#             Use a KMS-backed secret manager.
# Generate:   openssl rand -hex 32
#
QODIQA_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<REPLACE_WITH_64_HEX_CHARACTER_KEK>

# ── Execution Invariant Secret ───────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    HMAC-SHA256 key used to cryptographically bind the execution
#             invariant context (verifiedCtx) minted by the /ai/chat
#             enforcement pipeline and checked by services/execution-guard.js
#             before any provider call. Prevents a forged-but-correctly-shaped
#             verifiedCtx from passing the invariant check.
# Required:   YES
# Format:     Exactly 64 hexadecimal characters (256 bits / 32 bytes).
# Security:   Critical — a leaked value lets an attacker mint a valid
#             verifiedCtx and bypass policy/consent enforcement entirely.
#             Must be a DIFFERENT value from QODIQA_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY —
#             purpose isolation between artifact-signing and execution-
#             invariant secrets is a deliberate security boundary.
# Generate:   openssl rand -hex 32
#
QODIQA_EXECUTION_INVARIANT_SECRET=<REPLACE_WITH_64_HEX_CHARACTER_SECRET>

# ── Signing Identities (TRUST-1 remediation) ──────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Authenticating POST /sign requests.
# Required:   YES — but NOT an environment variable. There is no shared secret
#             to set here. Every caller of POST /sign authenticates with its
#             own individually-issued, individually-revocable signing API key
#             (Authorization: Bearer sk_<64 hex chars>), resolved via
#             src/core/signing/signingIdentityStore.js. Provision the first
#             signing identity after deployment with:
#               node scripts/provision-signing-identity.js --tenant-id <id> \
#                 --name <name> --environment production
#             which prints the sk_... key exactly once. Issue additional
#             identities via POST /signing-identities/:id/api-key. See
#             QUICKSTART.md's "Provision a Signing Identity" section and
#             docs/configuration/reference.md.
#
# (nothing to set here)

# ── OpenAI API Key ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Authentication for the OpenAI chat completions API.
# Required:   YES
# Format:     Starts with "sk-" — obtain from https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
# Security:   High — exposure incurs billing charges on your OpenAI account.
# Note:       OPENAI_BASE_URL is ignored — the canonical base URL is hardcoded
#             in the provider module to prevent SSRF.
# Note: managed by QODIQA_CONFIG_SECRETS_PROVIDER when set to a
#             non-"env" value — see the section near the top of this file.
#
# (decision D-E): EVERY provider credential is validated at boot for
#             placeholders and provider-specific format ('sk-' + >= 40 chars
#             for OpenAI, 'sk-ant-' for Anthropic, ...). A placeholder or
#             malformed key leaves that provider disabled and refuses a
#             production boot. Use any ONE valid provider; comment this out
#             if you use another (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY,
#             MISTRAL_API_KEY, LLAMA_BASE_URL, LOCAL_BASE_URL).
OPENAI_API_KEY=<REPLACE_WITH_REAL_OPENAI_API_KEY>

# ── Client Identifier ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Identifies this QODIQA deployment in signed artifacts and the
#             key store.  Used to resolve the active signing key.
# Required:   YES
# Format:     Alphanumeric string; 1–64 characters matching [A-Za-z0-9_-].
#             Use a meaningful identifier like your organization name or
#             service name (e.g. "acmecorp-prod", "payments-gateway").
# Security:   Not a secret — appears in artifact headers and audit logs.
#             Do not use generic values like "test", "demo", or "default".
#
QODIQA_CLIENT_ID=<REPLACE_WITH_MEANINGFUL_DEPLOYMENT_IDENTIFIER>

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BOOT VALIDATION REQUIREMENTS
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Every rule, with its production/development/test outcome and exact message:
#   docs/configuration/boot-rules.md   (generated from the validator itself)
# A production boot with ANY violation exits non-zero with one FATAL
# BOOT_VALIDATION_FATAL event listing every violation; it never starts degraded.
# After a successful boot, the INFO BOOT_REPORT event states what the process
# actually established (PostgreSQL/Redis TLS, migrations, keys, providers,
# rate limiting, planes, semantic fingerprint).

# ── DSR erasure pseudonymisation pepper ──────────────────────────────────────
# Required:   YES in production (or QODIQA_SIGNING_SECRET). >= 32 random chars.
# Generate:   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
QODIQA_DSR_PSEUDONYM_PEPPER=<REPLACE_WITH_64_HEX_CHARACTER_PEPPER>

# ── MAC keyring ──
# Purpose:    HMAC keys (encrypted under QODIQA_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY) that sign
#             consent-revocation and organisation-status pub/sub messages, the
#             org-status Redis hash and every consent-cache entry. Signing is
#             mandatory in every profile: unsigned or tampered messages are
#             rejected and a tampered cache entry is a miss.
# Required:   YES in production — the file must exist before boot; production
#             never generates one per node. Defaults to mac-keys.json beside
#             QODIQA_KEYS_PATH. Provision ONCE per deployment and share the file
#             with every node, like the signing keystore:
#               node scripts/security/macKeyring.js init
#             Rotate with `node scripts/security/macKeyring.js rotate` or
#             POST /key-admin/mac-keys/rotate (the previous key stays verifiable).
# Deprecated: REVOCATION_SIGNING_KEY / ORG_STATUS_SIGNING_KEY are ignored (boot warns).
QODIQA_MAC_KEYS_PATH=<REPLACE_WITH_ABSOLUTE_PATH_TO_MAC_KEYS_JSON>

# ── HTTP rate limiting ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required:   YES in production: RL_ENABLED=true AND REDIS_URL set (rediss://).
#             (Production refuses to boot with the limiter off.)
RL_ENABLED=true

# ── Admin and diagnostics planes ───────────────────────
# Two INDEPENDENT switches, both default OFF in production:
#   QODIQA_ADMIN_ENABLED        every RBAC-gated admin route (/audit, /rbac,
#                               /key-admin, /consent, /v1/admin/*, ...)
#   QODIQA_DIAGNOSTICS_ENABLED  /metrics, /diagnostics, /diagnostics/profile
# A disabled plane answers 404 (after authentication).,
# QODIQA_DIAGNOSTICS_ENABLED no longer opens the admin plane — a deployment that
# relied on it must add QODIQA_ADMIN_ENABLED=true (boot emits INFO
# ADMIN_PLANE_DISABLED while the admin plane is off). Enabling either plane in
# production requires QODIQA_OPS_TOKEN (>= 32 random characters).
# QODIQA_ADMIN_ENABLED=true
# QODIQA_DIAGNOSTICS_ENABLED=false

# ── Boot report file (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# When set, the BOOT_REPORT JSON (secret-free) is also written to this path.
# QODIQA_BOOT_REPORT_PATH=/var/lib/qodiqa/boot-report.json

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# POSTGRESQL / CONSENT REGISTRY
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Deployment Mode ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Single authoritative deployment-topology switch. When set,
#             resolves PostgreSQL enablement, audit-chain ordering mode,
#             migration verification, and multi-instance safety automatically
#             — takes precedence over QODIQA_PG_ENABLED / QODIQA_AUDIT_
#             ORDERING_MODE below when both are present. See
#             src/infra/config/deploymentMode.js and
#             docs/protocol/AUDIT-SCHEMA-V8.md.
# Required:   NO — recommended for new production deployments; existing
#             deployments using the legacy QODIQA_PG_ENABLED /
#             QODIQA_AUDIT_ORDERING_MODE vars below continue to work
#             unchanged if this is left unset.
# Values:     single-node | distributed
#
# QODIQA_DEPLOYMENT_MODE=distributed

# ── PostgreSQL Enable (legacy — superseded by QODIQA_DEPLOYMENT_MODE above) ───
# Purpose:    Enables the PostgreSQL connection pool.
# Required:   YES in practice — the default policies.json ships with
#             POL-001 (REQUIRE_CONSENT) enabled, and the Runtime Consent Registry
#             (src/core/consent/) is backed by PostgreSQL as its authoritative
#             store. With this unset, every consent-gated request BLOCKs with
#             CONSENT_STORE_UNAVAILABLE (fail-closed) — this is not a bug, it is
#             the intended fail-closed behavior when PostgreSQL is not configured.
#             Also enables PostgreSQL dual-write for the audit chain, plus the
#             transparency log, evidence chain, compliance, certification, and
#             attestation subsystems. See QUICKSTART.md's "Provision Consent
#             (PostgreSQL)" section and docs/configuration/reference.md §6.
# Required:   NO (default: false) — but see above. Ignored when
#             QODIQA_DEPLOYMENT_MODE is set (that variable takes precedence).
# Values:     true | false
#
QODIQA_PG_ENABLED=true

# ── PostgreSQL Connection ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Connection parameters read by src/infra/postgres/pool.js.
# Required:   YES when QODIQA_PG_ENABLED=true (either QODIQA_PG_URL alone, or
#             the QODIQA_PG_HOST/PORT/DATABASE/USER/PASSWORD group below).
#
# QODIQA_PG_URL=postgresql://<user>:<password>@<host>:5432/<database>?sslmode=require
QODIQA_PG_HOST=<REPLACE_WITH_POSTGRES_HOSTNAME>
QODIQA_PG_PORT=5432
QODIQA_PG_DATABASE=qodiqa
# Note: QODIQA_PG_PASSWORD/_MIG_PASSWORD/_RETENTION_PASSWORD below are
# managed by QODIQA_CONFIG_SECRETS_PROVIDER when set to a non-"env" value —
# see the section near the top of this file.
# (K7-D1): deploy/docker/docker-compose.prod.yml ONLY — the PostgreSQL cluster bootstrap superuser is a
# separate identity from the app role (QODIQA_PG_USER). The gateway never reads these; compose refuses to start
# without QODIQA_PG_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD. Uncomment and set them in the compose env file:
# QODIQA_PG_BOOTSTRAP_USER=postgres
# QODIQA_PG_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=<strong random superuser password>
QODIQA_PG_USER=<REPLACE_WITH_POSTGRES_USERNAME>
QODIQA_PG_PASSWORD=<REPLACE_WITH_STRONG_RANDOM_POSTGRES_PASSWORD>

# ── QODIQA_PG_ALLOW_SHARED — NEVER PUT THIS IN A .env FILE ──────────────
# Purpose:    One-shot override letting `npm run dev`'s bootstrap provision
#             against a database it did not itself generate. See
#             scripts/lib/bootstrapIsolationGuard.js.
# Required:   NO. Omit it entirely in production — it has no production role
#             whatsoever; it exists only for the local dev-bootstrap path.
#
# HARD CONSTRAINT: this variable must never appear in .env (or any other
# dotenv-loaded file), and the guard REFUSES TO START if it finds it there —
# on presence alone, whatever the value. Reason: dotenv would then supply the
# override to every future bootstrap, including the isolated witness sessions
# the guard exists to protect. That silent backfill is the exact mechanism that
# caused (an isolated session's boot reached the shared `qodiqa` database
# because PowerShell dropped an empty QODIQA_PG_DATABASE and dotenv filled it
# back in from .env). An override that can be inherited is a disabled guard.
#
# Correct usage — inline, for one invocation only:
#     QODIQA_PG_ALLOW_SHARED=true npm run dev
#
# Related: leaving QODIQA_PG_DATABASE UNSET is what gives each `npm run dev` a
# fresh, isolated per-boot database. Setting it in .env points every dev boot at
# one shared database and is what made reachable.

# ── PostgreSQL: dedicated retention role ──────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Credential for qodiqa_retention — a separate, least-privilege role
#             (migration 038_retention_dedicated_role.sql) used only by the
#             automated audit-retention cleanup cycle. Never reuse
#             QODIQA_PG_PASSWORD's value here — this must be a distinct
#             credential for a distinct role. See SECRETS_MANAGEMENT.md.
QODIQA_PG_RETENTION_USER=qodiqa_retention
QODIQA_PG_RETENTION_PASSWORD=<REPLACE_WITH_STRONG_RANDOM_POSTGRES_PASSWORD>

# ── PostgreSQL: dedicated cross-tenant roles ───────────────
# Purpose:    The ONLY sanctioned cross-tenant readers. The request-serving role
#             (QODIQA_PG_USER / qodiqa_app) can no longer call any cross-tenant
#             SECURITY DEFINER function (migration 061).
#               qodiqa_system     integrity + background work (audit-chain tail and
#                                 verifier, transparency root scheduler and gap
#                                 scanner, tenant-config hydration, emergency
#                                 sweep, GDPR erase function)
#               qodiqa_reporting  RBAC-gated operator consoles (audit store,
#                                 alerts, compliance, evidence chain, audit viewer)
#             Neither role has BYPASSRLS or any table grant. Both are REQUIRED
#             while QODIQA_PG_ENABLED=true (boot rule secret.pg_passwords); each
#             must be a distinct strong credential. Rotate the migration's
#             placeholder password (ALTER ROLE ... PASSWORD) before first use.
QODIQA_PG_SYSTEM_USER=qodiqa_system
QODIQA_PG_SYSTEM_PASSWORD=<REPLACE_WITH_STRONG_RANDOM_POSTGRES_PASSWORD>
QODIQA_PG_REPORTING_USER=qodiqa_reporting
QODIQA_PG_REPORTING_PASSWORD=<REPLACE_WITH_STRONG_RANDOM_POSTGRES_PASSWORD>

# ── PostgreSQL: control-plane role ──────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    qodiqa_control (migration 065) reads and writes ONLY
#             control_plane_records (066): signing identities, operator users
#             and custom roles, tenants, organizations, tenant-portal users,
#             the policy state and the signing-key status timeline. Every
#             replica pulls changes from PostgreSQL and refuses to answer from a
#             snapshot older than 5 s, so a revocation anywhere holds everywhere
#             within 5 s. REQUIRED in production (boot rule control_plane);
#             production never runs the dev/test memory mode. Rotate the
#             migration's placeholder password (ALTER ROLE ... PASSWORD) first.
QODIQA_PG_CONTROL_USER=qodiqa_control
QODIQA_PG_CONTROL_PASSWORD=<REPLACE_WITH_STRONG_RANDOM_POSTGRES_PASSWORD>

# ── PostgreSQL: migration-role placeholder-credential gate ─────────
# Purpose:    At boot, attempt a login as every migration-created role
#             (qodiqa_app/_read/_mig/_retention/_system/_reporting/_control/
#             _backup) with the placeholder password published in the
#             migrations; refuse to start if any login succeeds. Production
#             accepts only 'enforce' (the default). Rotate or NOLOGIN every
#             role first: node scripts/db/role-credentials.js --check
#             (docs/operations/database-role-management.md, "Placeholder credentials").
QODIQA_PG_ROLE_CREDENTIAL_GATE=enforce

# ── PostgreSQL: dedicated migration role ────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Credential for qodiqa_mig (migration 002_security_hardening.sql)
#             — a role with schema-modifying DDL rights that QODIQA_PG_USER's
#             request-serving role (qodiqa_app) does not have. Read only by
#             src/infra/postgres/migrate.js, never by pool.js. Never reuse
#             QODIQA_PG_PASSWORD's value here. See SECRETS_MANAGEMENT.md.
# NOT part of the gateway's environment. The running
#             service never uses the migration credential, and a production
#             boot REFUSES it (secret.pg_migration_credential_absent). Give it
#             only to the separate, one-shot migration job / operator shell:
#               QODIQA_PG_MIG_USER=qodiqa_mig QODIQA_PG_MIG_PASSWORD=<secret> \
#                 node src/infra/postgres/migrate.js
# QODIQA_PG_MIG_USER=qodiqa_mig
# QODIQA_PG_MIG_PASSWORD=<REPLACE_WITH_STRONG_RANDOM_POSTGRES_PASSWORD>

# ── PostgreSQL TLS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Enable TLS for PostgreSQL connections.
# Required:   NO (default: true when NODE_ENV=production, false otherwise)
# Values:     true | false
# Security:   Leave enabled in production. See QODIQA_PG_SSL_CA/CERT/KEY in
#             docs/configuration/reference.md for mTLS.
#
QODIQA_PG_SSL=true

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REDIS CONFIGURATION
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Redis Connection URL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Redis connection string for all three stores.
# Required:   NO (default: redis://localhost:6379)
# Format:     redis://host:port  or  rediss://host:port
#             Use rediss:// for TLS (recommended for production).
#             Credentials may be embedded: rediss://user:pass@host:port
#             Prefer explicit REDIS_USERNAME/REDIS_PASSWORD over embedded creds.
#
REDIS_URL=rediss://<REPLACE_WITH_REDIS_HOSTNAME>:6380

# ── Redis Authentication ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Credentials for Redis ACL authentication.
# Required:   NO (but strongly recommended in production)
# Format:     Any string.  Minimum 16 characters for REDIS_PASSWORD in production.
# Security:   High — exposure allows direct Redis manipulation.
# Generate:   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Note: REDIS_USERNAME/REDIS_PASSWORD below are managed by
# QODIQA_CONFIG_SECRETS_PROVIDER when set to a non-"env" value — see the
# section near the top of this file.
REDIS_USERNAME=<REPLACE_WITH_REDIS_ACL_USERNAME>
REDIS_PASSWORD=<REPLACE_WITH_STRONG_RANDOM_REDIS_PASSWORD>

# ── Redis TLS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Enable TLS for Redis connections regardless of URL scheme.
# Required:   NO (TLS is also enabled by using rediss:// in REDIS_URL)
# Values:     true | false
#
REDIS_TLS=true

# ── Redis stall detection ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    node-redis stops timing a command once it is written, so a SILENT
#             partition (socket open, no replies) used to hang every request and
#             leave /ready green. Every command now fails closed after
#             REDIS_COMMAND_DEADLINE_MS; a PING watchdog runs every
#             REDIS_WATCHDOG_INTERVAL_MS; after REDIS_STALL_THRESHOLD consecutive
#             misses the connection is torn down (readiness drops) and re-opened.
# Required:   NO (defaults 2000 / 2000 / 2). Production refuses 0 for either interval.
REDIS_COMMAND_DEADLINE_MS=2000
REDIS_WATCHDOG_INTERVAL_MS=2000
REDIS_STALL_THRESHOLD=2

# ── Audit anchoring: witnesses + customer-held anchors ─────────────────
# Purpose:    Makes insider rewrites / rollback / re-signed checkpoints of the audit
#             history DETECTABLE (boot check + offline verifier). Each signed
#             checkpoint is co-signed by a witness in another trust domain and
#             appended to an anchor file on storage the gateway does not control
#             alone. See docs/security/audit-anchoring.md.
# Required: YES in production: the boot is REFUSED without an anchor export, a witness
#             (local|exec) whose key is in the trusted set, and the witness public keys. There is NO waiver.
#             Distributed mode has no checkpoints/anchors yet and is REFUSED in production.
QODIQA_WITNESS_MODE=exec
QODIQA_WITNESS_ID=customer-witness
QODIQA_WITNESS_EXEC=<REPLACE_WITNESS_EXEC_PATH>               # e.g. an ssh/agent wrapper to the witness host
QODIQA_WITNESS_PUBLIC_KEYS=<REPLACE_WITNESS_PUBLIC_KEYS_PATH>  # witness public keys, delivered out of band
QODIQA_ANCHOR_EXPORT_PATH=<REPLACE_ANCHOR_EXPORT_PATH>        # customer-held storage, e.g. /mnt/customer-anchors/qodiqa-anchors.jsonl
# Checkpoint cadence (defaults tightened by was 10,000 entries / 24 h).
# QODIQA_AUDIT_CHECKPOINT_EVERY=1000
# QODIQA_AUDIT_CHECKPOINT_MAX_AGE_MS=900000

# ── Redis TLS Certificates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    TLS CA and client certificate paths for mTLS deployments.
# Required:   NO (optional; required for mTLS)
# Format:     Absolute filesystem path to PEM-encoded certificate files.
# Note:       REDIS_TLS_CERT and REDIS_TLS_KEY must both be set or both absent.
# Security:   REDIS_TLS_KEY path points to a private key — restrict to mode 400.
# Note:/F29-class: these three used to be active by default with
#             fake, non-existent paths. src/infra/secrets/secretValidation.js
#             checks file-existence for these unconditionally (independent of
#             REDIS_TLS's own value) and — for `npm start`/server.js
#             specifically — treats a missing file as FATAL
#             (ENV_VALIDATION_FATAL, process.exit), not merely the WARN
#             `npm run dev`'s bootstrap gets for the same condition; commented
#             out here also unblocks `npm start`, not just local Redis TLS.
#             Uncomment and point at real certificate files for a genuine
#             mTLS deployment.
#
# REDIS_TLS_CA=/etc/qodiqa-tls/ca.crt
# REDIS_TLS_CERT=/etc/qodiqa-tls/client.crt
# REDIS_TLS_KEY=/etc/qodiqa-tls/client.key

# ── Redis TLS Certificate Verification ────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Controls whether the Redis server certificate is verified.
# Required:   NO (default: verification ENABLED)
# Values:     true (default) | false (UNSAFE — never use in production)
# Security:   CRITICAL — setting to 'false' disables TLS certificate verification
#             and makes TLS trivially bypassable.  Leave unset or set to 'true'.
#
# REDIS_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=false   # DO NOT SET IN PRODUCTION

# ── Redis Connection Timeout ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Milliseconds to wait for a Redis connection at startup.
# Required:   NO (default: 5000)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–60000.
#
REDIS_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REPLAY PROTECTION
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Strict Replay Protection ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Refuse to start if Redis persistence guarantees are insufficient.
#             When true, QODIQA validates that Redis has AOF enabled with
#             appendfsync=everysec (or always) before accepting traffic.
# Required:   NO (default: false)
# Values:     true | false
# Recommendation: Set to 'true' in all production deployments.
# Note: this line used to be active by default. scripts/lib/
#             devEnvironment.js's own local-dev relaxation
#             (`if (redisReachable && process.env.STRICT_REPLAY_PROTECTION
#             === undefined) { process.env.STRICT_REPLAY_PROTECTION =
#             'false'; }`) only fires when this variable is genuinely unset —
#             an explicit `true` here (as shipped) permanently defeats it,
#             making QUICKSTART.md's own claim that `npm run dev` "relaxes
#             STRICT_REPLAY_PROTECTION's stricter AOF-durability startup
#             check by default" false for anyone who copies this template
#             and leaves it at its default, exactly as the guide instructs.
#             Live-confirmed: causes a FATAL STRICT_REPLAY_PROTECTION_
#             VIOLATION startup abort against a plain (non-AOF) local Redis.
#             Left commented out so the app's own designed dev-mode
#             relaxation applies; set explicitly to 'true' for production.
# See:
#
# STRICT_REPLAY_PROTECTION=true

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SERVER CONFIGURATION
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Runtime Environment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Controls production-mode behavior.
#             In production, env validation failures cause process.exit(1).
#             In other environments, failures emit warnings and startup continues.
# Required:   NO (but strongly recommended to set to 'production')
# Values:     production | development | test
#
NODE_ENV=production

# ── HTTP Port ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Port the HTTP server listens on.
# Required:   NO (default: 3000)
# Format:     Integer, 1–65535.  Use a non-privileged port (>1024).
#
PORT=3000

# ── Request Body Size Limit ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Global maximum request body size in bytes.
# Required:   NO (default: 65536 — 64 KiB)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–104857600 (100 MiB maximum).
#
MAX_BODY_BYTES=65536

# ── Allowed CORS Origins ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Comma-separated list of browser origins permitted to make
#             cross-origin requests to QODIQA.  Any origin not in this list
#             receives no CORS headers and is blocked by the browser.
# Required:   NO (but strongly recommended in production)
# Format:     Comma-separated full origins: scheme://host[:port].
#             No wildcards, no trailing slashes, no paths.
# Example:    https://portal.example.com,https://admin.example.com
# Security:   Wildcard ("*") is never permitted.  Omitting this variable in
#             production causes a startup WARN and denies all cross-origin
#             browser requests (fail-closed behavior — safe but restrictive).
#             Setting malformed values causes process.exit(1) at startup.
# Note: this line used to be active by default with two
#             literal <REPLACE_WITH_...> placeholders — src/infra/cors/
#             corsPolicy.js's own startup validator treats a malformed
#             origin as always-fatal (CORS_VALIDATION_FATAL, unconditional
#             process.exit(1), independent of whether any browser request
#             ever arrives), so `npm run dev`/`npm start` could never boot
#             with this template's own shipped default. Left commented out —
#             corsPolicy.js's own documented behavior for an unset value in
#             a non-production NODE_ENV is "no findings (dev defaults
#             used)," i.e. this is the safe, designed local-dev default, not
#             a workaround. Uncomment and set real origins for production.
#
# QODIQA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://<REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_PORTAL_DOMAIN>,https://<REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_ADMIN_DOMAIN>

# ── Trusted Proxy ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Comma-separated list of trusted proxy identifiers for
#             X-Forwarded-For processing.  Used to determine the real client IP.
# Required: NO
# Format:     Comma-separated list of Express trust proxy values.
#             Examples: loopback | 10.0.0.0/8 | 10.0.0.1,10.0.0.2
# Security:   Only list proxies you control.  Listing 0.0.0.0/0 allows any
#             IP to spoof X-Forwarded-For headers; production refuses any
#             trust-all entry (true, *, 0.0.0.0/0, ::/0), and also refuses whole
#             private ranges (uniquelocal, linklocal, 10.0.0.0/8 ...) unless
#             QODIQA_TRUSTED_PROXY_BROAD_ACK=true acknowledges that a
#             NetworkPolicy/firewall lets ONLY the proxy reach the gateway.
#             One list for everything: Express trust proxy, req.clientIp and the
#             admin IP allowlist (QODIQA_OPS_TRUST_PROXY is deprecated and ignored).
#             Kubernetes / cloud load balancer: list your ingress or LB CIDR
#             explicitly, e.g. TRUSTED_PROXY=loopback,10.42.0.0/16
#
TRUSTED_PROXY=loopback
#
# TRUSTED_PROXY_REAL_IP — honour X-Real-IP from a trusted proxy when it sends no X-Forwarded-For. Off by
# default: set true only if your proxy OVERWRITES X-Real-IP (a proxy that passes a client-supplied value through
# would let the client choose its IP). X-Forwarded-For (sent by ingress-nginx and most proxies) is unaffected.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_REAL_IP=false

# ── Credentials: lockout, MFA, API-key expiry ───────────────────────────
# QODIQA_SIGN_AUTH_LOCKOUT_ENABLED  /sign auth-failure bucket (Redis, fails closed).
#             Default on; production refuses false. Tuning:
#             QODIQA_SIGN_AUTH_FAILURE_MAX (10), QODIQA_SIGN_AUTH_FAILURE_WINDOW_MS
#             (60000), QODIQA_SIGN_AUTH_LOCKOUT_MS (300000).
# QODIQA_OPS_MFA_REQUIRED  operator TOTP step-up (header X-QODIQA-OTP) on privileged
#             mutations (/rbac, /key-admin, /signing-identities, /policy-ui,
#             /v1/admin/*, /v1/webhooks, /v1/prq, governance approve/publish).
#             Default true; production refuses false. Operators enrol with
#             POST /rbac/me/mfa/enroll then POST /rbac/me/mfa/confirm.
# QODIQA_API_KEY_TTL_DAYS              operator + signing key lifetime (90; production max 365)
# QODIQA_API_KEY_ROTATION_GRACE_HOURS  previous key valid after rotation (24)
# QODIQA_API_KEY_EXPIRY_WARNING_DAYS   X-QODIQA-Key-Expires + WARN event window (14)
#
# QODIQA_SIGN_AUTH_LOCKOUT_ENABLED=true
# QODIQA_OPS_MFA_REQUIRED=true
# QODIQA_API_KEY_TTL_DAYS=90

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# FILE PATHS
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Key Store Path ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Filesystem path to the encrypted signing key store (keys.json).
# Required:   YES in production — boot validation refuses a production process
#             whose keystore is unset or resolves inside the application
#             directory (docs/configuration/boot-rules.md, "Retained").
#             Development may leave it unset (data/keys.json relative to the
#             project root). this line used to be active by default with
#             a hardcoded /opt/qodiqa/data/keys.json — a real absolute path
#             that is meaningless on Windows (Node resolves a leading "/"
#             against the current drive root, silently landing at
#             C:\opt\qodiqa\data\keys.json — one fixed, machine-wide
#             location shared by every clone and every session on that
#             machine, completely outside this repo's own isolation model).
#             Hence no hardcoded path here: replace the placeholder with a real
#             absolute path on a dedicated secret volume of the host you control.
# Security:   Directory must be mode 700, file must be mode 600, owned by
#             the QODIQA process user.
#
QODIQA_KEYS_PATH=<REPLACE_WITH_ABSOLUTE_PATH_OUTSIDE_THE_APPLICATION_DIRECTORY>

# ── Transparency signing-key file ───────────────────────
# Required:   YES, in production — boot validation refuses an unset value or a path inside the application
#             directory. The audit-checkpoint key file (checkpoint-keys.json) defaults beside it.
# Security:   Same secret volume as QODIQA_KEYS_PATH; directory mode 700, file mode 600.
QODIQA_TRANSPARENCY_KEYS_PATH=<REPLACE_WITH_ABSOLUTE_PATH_TO_TRANSPARENCY_KEYS_JSON>

# ── Transparency / persistence file journal ───────────────────────────
# Purpose:    While PostgreSQL is down, a transparency leaf that cannot be registered or queued is written to this
#             append-only, fsync'ed journal and replayed (marked payload.recovery.late, original order kept) once the
#             database is back. It is the only durable copy in that window.
# Required:   Set it in production. It MUST be a persistent volume outside the application directory (the same volume
#             as QODIQA_KEYS_PATH), never the container's ephemeral filesystem: boot validation refuses a path inside
#             the application directory or on a container's root filesystem, and warns when it is unset.
# Bound:      QODIQA_TRANSPARENCY_JOURNAL_MAX_UNAVAILABLE_MS (default 60000, 1000..3600000): how long neither the queue
#             nor this journal may be able to take a leaf before new EXECUTE decisions are refused (503) and /ready is 503.
QODIQA_PRQ_JOURNAL_DIR=<REPLACE_WITH_ABSOLUTE_PATH_ON_THE_PERSISTENT_VOLUME>

# ── External anchoring of audit checkpoints ───────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Pin each (daily) signed checkpoint outside the gateway's trust domain: a signed commit in an anchors git
#             repository, an OpenTimestamps stamp, an RFC 3161 token. QODIQA contacts no network and never pushes; the
#             repository must be mirrored where the gateway operator cannot rewrite it. Asynchronous, never blocks.
# Required:   NO (recommended). Configure at least one kind. See docs/configuration/reference.md section 20.
# QODIQA_ANCHOR_GIT_REPO=<REPLACE_WITH_PATH_OF_A_GIT_WORKING_TREE_OUTSIDE_THE_APPLICATION_DIRECTORY>
# QODIQA_ANCHOR_GIT_SIGN=true
# QODIQA_ANCHOR_OTS_EXEC=<REPLACE_WITH_PATH_OF_AN_OPENTIMESTAMPS_ADAPTER>
# QODIQA_ANCHOR_TSA_EXEC=<REPLACE_WITH_PATH_OF_AN_RFC3161_ADAPTER>
# QODIQA_ANCHOR_EXTERNAL_INTERVAL_MS=86400000

# ── Dedicated key-encryption keys for the audit key stores ────────────
# Purpose:    Give the transparency and checkpoint key files a KEK of their own, so one leaked KEK does not open the
#             signing keys of the artifact, transparency and checkpoint trust domains at once.
# Required:   NO. Unset = the store uses QODIQA_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY (compatible). Set = 64 hex characters, different
#             from every other KEK. Generate each with: openssl rand -hex 32
#             An existing key file stays readable only under the KEK that encrypted it (KEK_FINGERPRINT_MISMATCH
#             otherwise), so set these for a NEW deployment or provision that store afresh (move the old file
#             aside: its public keys must stay published so older signatures keep verifying).
# Enforce:    QODIQA_AUDIT_KEK_SEPARATION=off (default) | warn | enforce — in production, 'enforce' refuses to boot
#             unless the artifact, transparency and checkpoint KEKs are three different values.
# QODIQA_TRANSPARENCY_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<REPLACE_WITH_64_HEX_CHARS>
# QODIQA_CHECKPOINT_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<REPLACE_WITH_64_HEX_CHARS>
# QODIQA_AUDIT_KEK_SEPARATION=enforce

# ── Audit Log Path ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Filesystem path to the cryptographically chained audit log.
# Required:   NO (default: audit.log relative to project root — safe for
#             local development). same hardcoded-/opt/qodiqa
#             defect as QODIQA_KEYS_PATH above — left commented out here for
#             the same reason. Uncomment for a real production deployment.
# Security:   Directory must be mode 700, file must be mode 600.
#             Mount on persistent storage — audit log must survive pod restarts.
#
# QODIQA_AUDIT_LOG=/opt/qodiqa/logs/audit.log

# ── Policy File Path ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Filesystem path to the policy configuration file.
# Required: YES, in production — boot validation refuses
#             (BOOT_VALIDATION_FATAL, variable POLICY_FILE) if this is unset when
#             NODE_ENV=production; POLICY_FILE_REQUIRED remains as a second check.
#             Unlike QODIQA_KEYS_PATH/QODIQA_AUDIT_LOG above (genuinely
#             optional even in production — the repo-relative default is
#             safe for those), there is NO safe default here: the
#             repository-root policies.json this template's old commented-
#             out default pointed at is a demo/development fixture (an
#             always-on BLOCK_IP rule, demo-tuned rate limits) — this is
#             the exact variable CLAUDE.md's own incident names
#             ("unisolated POLICY_FILE leaked test config into real
#             policies.json"), and silently shipping its semantics as the
#             production default was's own root cause, not just an
#             undocumented gap.
# Action:     Copy production.policies.example (repository root) to the path
#             below, review and adjust every policy for your deployment,
#             then set this variable. Do not deploy production.policies.
#             example unedited — it is a documented template, not a
#             ready-to-use production policy set.
# Note:       Note: POLICY_FILE is import-only; SIGHUP re-reads the control plane, not this file.
#
POLICY_FILE=/opt/qodiqa/config/policies.json

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TIMEOUT CONFIGURATION
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Graceful Shutdown Timeout ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Maximum milliseconds to wait for graceful shutdown before forcing exit(1).
# Required:   NO (default: 30000)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–600000.
# Note:       Set Kubernetes terminationGracePeriodSeconds and ECS stopTimeout
#             to at least SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS/1000 + 15 seconds.
#
SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ── AI Chat Timeout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Maximum milliseconds for the full /ai/chat pipeline (enforce + execute).
# Required:   NO (default: 30000)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–600000.
#
AI_CHAT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ── Idempotency-Key support (API-2) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Master kill-switch for POST /ai/chat's Idempotency-Key header.
#             When false, the header is ignored entirely (treated as absent)
#             and every request behaves exactly as it did before this
#             remediation. Redis-backed — see src/core/idempotency/.
# Required:   NO (default: true)
# Format:     'true' | 'false'
#
IDEMPOTENCY_ENABLED=true

# Purpose:    Maximum accepted length, in characters, of the Idempotency-Key
#             header VALUE once it has passed the byte-size guard below.
# Required:   NO (default: 255)
# Format:     Positive integer.
#
IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_MAX_LENGTH=255

# Purpose:    Maximum accepted byte length of the raw Idempotency-Key header
#             value — checked before any further parsing, independent of
#             IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_MAX_LENGTH above.
# Required:   NO (default: 1024)
# Format:     Positive integer.
#
IDEMPOTENCY_HEADER_MAX_BYTES=1024

# Purpose:    TTL for an in-flight (pending) idempotency reservation — bounds
#             how long an abandoned/crashed reservation blocks a fresh retry
#             before self-expiring. Must comfortably exceed AI_CHAT_TIMEOUT_MS;
#             defaults to AI_CHAT_TIMEOUT_MS + 15000 automatically when unset.
# Required:   NO (default: AI_CHAT_TIMEOUT_MS + 15000)
# Format:     Positive integer, milliseconds.
#
# IDEMPOTENCY_RESERVATION_TTL_MS=45000

# Purpose:    Retention TTL for a durably-completed (successful) idempotency
#             record — how long a retry with the same Idempotency-Key
#             continues to replay the original response before the key
#             becomes eligible to execute again.
# Required:   NO (default: 86400000 — 24 hours)
# Format:     Positive integer, milliseconds.
#
IDEMPOTENCY_RETENTION_TTL_MS=86400000

# Purpose:    Maximum time a request waits for a CONCURRENT duplicate
#             (same tenant + Idempotency-Key + fingerprint, still executing)
#             to finish before returning 409 IDEMPOTENCY_REQUEST_IN_PROGRESS.
# Required:   NO (default: 10000)
# Format:     Positive integer, milliseconds. Should stay well under
#             AI_CHAT_TIMEOUT_MS so the waiting request can still respond
#             within its own client's timeout budget.
#
IDEMPOTENCY_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Purpose:    Poll interval while waiting for a concurrent duplicate (above).
# Required:   NO (default: 200)
# Format:     Positive integer, milliseconds.
#
IDEMPOTENCY_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=200

# ── OpenAI API Timeout ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Maximum milliseconds for a single OpenAI API call.
# Required:   NO (default: 30000)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–600000.
#
OPENAI_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ── Verify Endpoint Timeout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Maximum milliseconds for /verify/v2 policy evaluation.
# Required:   NO (default: 5000)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–600000.
#
VERIFY_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# ── Audit Endpoint Timeout ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Maximum milliseconds for the /audit/chain/integrity chain-walk endpoint.
# Required:   NO (default: 10000)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–600000.
#
AUDIT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# VERIFICATION LIMITS
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Artifact Header Size Limit ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Maximum bytes for the x-qodiqa-artifact header value.
# Required:   NO (default: 8192 — 8 KiB)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–1048576.
#
VERIFY_MAX_ARTIFACT_BYTES=8192

# ── Request Body Size Limit for /verify ──────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Maximum bytes for the /verify/v2 request body.
# Required:   NO (default: 4096 — 4 KiB)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–1048576.
#
VERIFY_MAX_REQUEST_BYTES=4096

# ── Object Nesting Depth Limit ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Maximum nesting depth for artifact and request objects.
#             Prevents DoS via deeply nested structures.
# Required:   NO (default: 12)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–64.
#
VERIFY_MAX_OBJECT_DEPTH=12

# ── Verify Rate Limit ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Maximum requests per window to /verify/v2.
# Required:   NO (default: 60)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–100000.
#
VERIFY_V2_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=60

# ── Verify Rate Limit Window ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Duration of the /verify/v2 rate limit window in milliseconds.
# Required:   NO (default: 60000 — 1 minute)
# Format:     Positive integer, 1–3600000.
#
VERIFY_V2_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# OPERATIONAL ENDPOINT AUTHENTICATION
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── RBAC (always enforced — see docs/security/RBAC_MODEL.md) ─────────────────
# RBAC authorization cannot be disabled. Every RBAC-gated endpoint (/rbac,
# /key-admin, /v1/admin/organizations, /v1/admin/tenants, /consent, /audit,
# /dashboard, and others — see docs/security/RBAC_MODEL.md for the full list)
# requires an authenticated req.rbacUser resolved from a real, explicit RBAC
# user record with an assigned role. There is no configuration that grants
# administrative access without one.
#
# First-time setup: run `npm run rbac:bootstrap-key` on the running instance
# (kubectl exec / docker exec / direct host access — never over the network)
# to provision the first admin account and print its API key exactly once.
# Use that key to create additional operator accounts via POST /rbac/users.
# See docs/security/RBAC_MODEL.md for the full bootstrap and operator
# provisioning workflow.

# ── Per-Operator API Keys (required for RBAC-gated endpoints) ────────────────
# Purpose:    Each operator receives an individual API key via the RBAC admin
#             API: POST /rbac/users/:id/api-key
#             Keys are stored as SHA-256 hashes (never plaintext).
#             Keys authenticate AND identify the operator — no shared identity.
#             This is the ONLY credential type that can satisfy an
#             RBAC-gated route (see below) — it is the sole source of
#             req.rbacUser.role.
# Required:   YES for any RBAC-gated route (see docs/security/RBAC_MODEL.md).
# Format:     ok_<64 hex characters>  (generated by the system, not set here)
# Issuance:   POST /rbac/users/:id/api-key  (requires MANAGE_USERS permission)
# Rotation:   Re-issue (POST again) — old key invalidated immediately
# Revocation: DELETE /rbac/users/:id/api-key — immediate
# Note:       Keys are passed via: Authorization: Bearer ok_<...>
#                               or: X-Ops-Token: ok_<...>

# ── Shared Ops Token (DEPRECATED — does NOT grant RBAC access) ────────────────
# Purpose:    Legacy single shared token. Authenticates requests to endpoints
#             that require ONLY ops authentication (/health, /ready, /metrics,
#             /diagnostics) but does NOT set req.rbacUser — it CANNOT satisfy
#             any RBAC-gated route (/rbac, /key-admin, /consent, etc.), by
#             design: a shared secret has no individual role to carry, and
#             granting one from it would reintroduce an implicit-admin path.
# Required: NO — use per-operator keys instead. production refuses to
#             boot WITHOUT it while QODIQA_ADMIN_ENABLED or
#             QODIQA_DIAGNOSTICS_ENABLED is true.
# Status:     DEPRECATED. Remove after all operators have migrated to API keys.
#             Emits OPS_AUTH_SHARED_TOKEN_USED WARN on every use.
# Migration:  1. Issue API keys to all operators via POST /rbac/users/:id/api-key
#             2. Update all operator credentials to use their individual key
#             3. Remove QODIQA_OPS_TOKEN from this config
#             4. Verify all ops access uses api-key authMethod in logs
#
# Note: managed by QODIQA_CONFIG_SECRETS_PROVIDER when set to a
#             non-"env" value — see the section near the top of this file.
# QODIQA_OPS_TOKEN=<REPLACE_WITH_32+_CHARACTER_RANDOM_SECRET>

# ── Ops IP Allowlist ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Restrict ops endpoints to specific IP ranges in addition to auth.
# Required:   NO (default: IP allowlisting disabled)
# Format:     Comma-separated IPv4 addresses or CIDR ranges.
# Example:    10.0.0.0/8,192.168.1.100
#
# QODIQA_OPS_IP_ALLOWLIST_ENABLED=true
# QODIQA_OPS_IP_ALLOWLIST=10.0.0.0/8,192.168.1.0/24

# ── Auth-Failure Lockout (AUTH-2 — see docs/security/AUTH_LOCKOUT.md) ─────────
# Purpose:    The ops rate limiter above throttles overall throughput but
#             treats a wrong credential no differently from a correct one —
#             this is a SEPARATE control that tracks consecutive
#             authentication FAILURES per source (independent of general
#             request volume) and applies escalating friction: exponential
#             backoff, then a temporary lockout. Makes brute-forcing the
#             shared QODIQA_OPS_TOKEN (or blind-guessing a per-operator API
#             key) impractical without affecting legitimate operators using
#             correct credentials from unrelated sources.
# Required:   NO (default: enabled, with the secure defaults below)
# Storage:    Redis-backed when REDIS_URL is set (required for correct
#             enforcement across multiple instances); falls back to a
#             single-process in-memory store otherwise (dev/test only).
#
QODIQA_OPS_AUTH_LOCKOUT_ENABLED=true
QODIQA_OPS_AUTH_LOCKOUT_MAX_FAILURES=5
QODIQA_OPS_AUTH_LOCKOUT_DURATION_MS=900000
QODIQA_OPS_AUTH_LOCKOUT_BACKOFF_MIN_MS=250
QODIQA_OPS_AUTH_LOCKOUT_BACKOFF_MAX_MS=5000
# QODIQA_OPS_AUTH_LOCKOUT_FAILURE_WINDOW_MS=900000

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BACKUP SCHEDULING
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# These are consumed by scripts/pg-backup.sh and scripts/dr/*.js, invoked on
# a schedule via deploy/kubernetes/base/backup-cronjobs.yaml (Kubernetes) or
# deploy/systemd/*.timer (self-hosted) — NOT by the qodiqa server process
# itself. Irrelevant if you don't run either scheduling mechanism.

# ── Retention (count-based, tiered) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    How many backups each tier keeps before older ones are pruned.
# Required:   NO (defaults shown)
#
QODIQA_BACKUP_RETAIN_DAILY=7
QODIQA_BACKUP_RETAIN_WEEKLY=4
QODIQA_BACKUP_RETAIN_MONTHLY=12
QODIQA_DR_RETAIN_DAILY=7
QODIQA_DR_RETAIN_WEEKLY=4
QODIQA_DR_RETAIN_MONTHLY=12

# ── Backup integrity seal ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    HMAC-SHA256 key sealing scripts/pg-backup.sh's dump files.
# Required:   NO, but omitting it means the seal is a plain SHA-256 (still
#             detects corruption, not tampering by someone with write access
#             to the backup volume).
# Generate:   openssl rand -hex 32
#
QODIQA_BACKUP_HMAC_KEY=<REPLACE_WITH_32+_CHAR_SECRET>

# ── Backup/DR failure alerting ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Slack/PagerDuty-compatible webhook posted to on any scheduled
#             backup, verification, or restore-validation failure.
# Required:   NO — every script no-ops safely if unset.
# Security:   Treat as sensitive — most webhook URLs embed a bearer-token-like
#             path segment.
#
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/REPLACE/WITH/REAL

# ── Metrics ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Prometheus textfile-collector output path for scheduled backup
#             jobs (see scripts/dr/metricsFile.js). Point node_exporter's
#             textfile collector at this same directory.
# Required:   NO — omitting it just skips metrics file emission.
#
QODIQA_BACKUP_METRICS_FILE=/var/backups/qodiqa/metrics/qodiqa_pg_backup.prom

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TRANSPARENCY GAP SCANNER (CLN-04 — automated log integrity detection)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# GapScanner (src/core/transparency/scanner/) performs genuine sequence/hash/
# root verification against the transparency log in PostgreSQL — real
# detection, not a stub — but is DISABLED BY DEFAULT. With PostgreSQL
# enabled and this left unset, transparency_log_entries/transparency_roots
# get NO automated gap, hash-chain, or Merkle-root integrity checking; a
# splice or corruption is only caught by a manual scan
# (POST /transparency/admin/scan) or ad hoc SQL — see
# docs/operations/transparency-recovery.md §3.1.
#
# Required: NO (defaults to disabled) — startup only WARNS when PG is
#           enabled and this is unset; see NO_TRANSPARENCY_SCANNER_CONFIGURED.
#
# TRANSPARENCY_SCANNER_ENABLED=true
#
# Set QODIQA_REQUIRE_TRANSPARENCY_SCANNER=true to make startup FAIL CLOSED
# (process.exit(1)) instead of just warning when PG is enabled and the
# scanner is not — recommended wherever transparency-log integrity is a
# compliance requirement, mirroring QODIQA_REQUIRE_NOTIFICATION_CHANNEL below.
#
# QODIQA_REQUIRE_TRANSPARENCY_SCANNER=false
#
# Other scanner knobs (interval, mode, batch size, retries — see
# src/core/transparency/scanner/scannerConfig.js for the full list and
# defaults):
# TRANSPARENCY_SCANNER_INTERVAL=5m
# TRANSPARENCY_SCANNER_MODE=INCREMENTAL

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TRANSPARENCY ROOT SCHEDULER
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Started at boot whenever PostgreSQL is enabled (server/startup.js §6.6b).
# Each signed root covers every leaf appended since the previous one; a
# decision's inclusion proof (GET /transparency/proof/<transparencyId>, the
# inclusionProofRef in every /ai/chat response) resolves once a root covers
# it. Multi-replica safe (roots head lock; a losing replica skips).
#
# QODIQA_TRANSPARENCY_ROOT_SCHEDULER_ENABLED=true   # 'false' opts out
# TRANSPARENCY_ROOT_INTERVAL_MS=60000               # time trigger (0 = off)
# TRANSPARENCY_ROOT_ENTRY_THRESHOLD=0               # also root after N new leaves (0 = off)
# TRANSPARENCY_ROOT_INITIAL_DELAY_MS=60000          # first run when no root exists yet

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PERSISTENCE QUEUES — depth monitor and alert thresholds
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# src/core/persistenceRecovery/queueMonitor.js samples every durable backlog
# into gauges (qodiqa_prq_queue_depth{operation_type,status},
# qodiqa_prq_retrying_depth, qodiqa_prq_oldest_pending_age_seconds,
# qodiqa_prq_dlq_depth, qodiqa_prq_journal_depth, qodiqa_audit_mirror_writer_depth,
# qodiqa_transparency_unrooted_id_span) and raises one OPERATIONAL/QUEUE_BACKLOG
# alert per threshold crossing (re-armed after recovery). 0 disables an alert.
# A transparency write that fails on PostgreSQL after the provider call is
# queued (TRANSPARENCY_SAVE, filesystem journal if PG refuses the enqueue) and
# the response says transparency.persisted:"pending" — the journal directory
# (QODIQA_PRQ_JOURNAL_DIR) must be on durable storage.
#
# QODIQA_QUEUE_MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS=15000
# QODIQA_ALERT_PRQ_PENDING_DEPTH=500
# QODIQA_ALERT_PRQ_RETRYING_DEPTH=100
# QODIQA_ALERT_TRANSPARENCY_PENDING_DEPTH=100
# QODIQA_ALERT_PRQ_DLQ_DEPTH=1
# QODIQA_ALERT_PRQ_JOURNAL_DEPTH=1
# QODIQA_ALERT_AUDIT_MIRROR_WRITER_DEPTH=1000
# QODIQA_ALERT_TRANSPARENCY_UNROOTED_SPAN=50000

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# NOTIFICATION CHANNELS (MON-3 — operational alert delivery)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# QODIQA's alerting framework (src/alerting/
# and docs/operations/notification-setup.md) detects CRITICAL operational
# failures automatically: PostgreSQL/Redis unavailability, HA/multi-instance
# violations, audit chain corruption, signing/KMS provider failures, policy
# enforcement invariant violations, and more. Every channel below is DISABLED
# BY DEFAULT (secure-by-default — no outbound webhook fires until you
# configure a destination). With nothing configured here, QODIQA still
# DETECTS every one of these failures, but NOBODY IS NOTIFIED — alerts are
# only visible via GET /alerts, GET /alerts/stream (SSE), or your own log
# aggregation.
#
# Configure AT LEAST ONE channel below for production deployments. You do
# not need to configure all seven — pick what fits your on-call tooling.
# Multiple channels may be enabled simultaneously; QODIQA dispatches to every
# enabled channel that matches the alert's severity (see SeverityModel.js —
# CRITICAL fans out to all 7, LOW only to Webhook+SIEM).
#
# Set QODIQA_REQUIRE_NOTIFICATION_CHANNEL=true to make startup FAIL CLOSED
# (process.exit(1)) instead of just warning when zero channels are
# configured — recommended for regulated/compliance-sensitive deployments
# where "alerts nobody sees" is not an acceptable startup state.
#
# QODIQA_REQUIRE_NOTIFICATION_CHANNEL=false
#
# Note: every credential in this NOTIFICATION CHANNELS section
# (ALERT_WEBHOOK_SECRET, ALERT_SIEM_TOKEN, ALERT_PAGERDUTY_ROUTING_KEY,
# ALERT_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL, ALERT_TEAMS_WEBHOOK_URL, ALERT_SMS_TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN,
# ALERT_EMAIL_SMTP_PASS, ALERT_EMAIL_SENDGRID_API_KEY,
# ALERT_EMAIL_MAILGUN_API_KEY) is managed by QODIQA_CONFIG_SECRETS_PROVIDER
# when set to a non-"env" value — see the section near the top of this file.

# ── Generic Webhook (Slack-compatible / custom receiver) ─────────────────────
# Purpose:    HMAC-signed JSON POST to any HTTP endpoint you control.
# Required:   NO (default: disabled)
# Format:     Full HTTPS URL.
# Security:   ALERT_WEBHOOK_SECRET signs every payload with HMAC-SHA256
#             (X-QODIQA-Signature header) — verify it on the receiving end.
# NOTE:       This SAME variable is also read by three other independent
#             call sites in this codebase, all designed as best-effort,
#             fire-and-forget notifiers that share one generic "ops webhook"
#             destination rather than requiring separate configuration:
#               1. src/alerting/notification/channels/WebhookChannel.js —
#                  the alerting framework's own webhook channel (this section).
#               2. scripts/dr/alert.js — backup/DR job failure alerts
#                  (see "Backup/DR failure alerting" above).
#               3. src/runtime/earlyCriticalNotify.js — best-effort CRITICAL
#                  notification for the multi-instance HA startup guard,
#                  fired before src/alerting has even initialized (see
#                  src/core/audit/multiInstanceGuard.js).
#             This is intentional, not a misconfiguration: point it at a
#             Slack/Teams-compatible incoming webhook or a generic collector
#             and all three producers will post to it. If you need to route
#             these to different destinations, fork the relevant channel to
#             read a distinct env var.
#
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/<REPLACE_WITH_REAL_PATH>
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<REPLACE_WITH_32+_CHAR_RANDOM_SECRET>
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_ENABLED=false

# ── Slack ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Slack Incoming Webhook (Block Kit formatted messages).
# Required:   NO (default: disabled)
# Format:     Full HTTPS URL from Slack's "Incoming Webhooks" app config.
# Setup:      https://api.slack.com/messaging/webhooks
#
# ALERT_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/<REPLACE_WITH_REAL_PATH>
# ALERT_SLACK_CHANNEL=#qodiqa-alerts
# ALERT_SLACK_USERNAME=QODIQA Alerting
# ALERT_SLACK_ENABLED=false

# ── Microsoft Teams ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Teams Incoming Webhook (Adaptive Card formatted messages).
# Required:   NO (default: disabled)
# Format:     Full HTTPS URL from a Teams channel connector.
# Setup:      Teams channel -> Connectors -> Incoming Webhook
#
# ALERT_TEAMS_WEBHOOK_URL=https://<REPLACE_WITH_TENANT>.webhook.office.com/webhookb2/<REPLACE>
# ALERT_TEAMS_ENABLED=false

# ── Email (SMTP, or SendGrid/Mailgun API) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Email delivery for alert notifications.
# Required:   NO (default: disabled)
# Format:     ALERT_EMAIL_PROVIDER selects the transport: smtp (default,
#             built-in raw-socket client, no dependency) | sendgrid | mailgun.
# Security:   Credentials below are secrets — never commit real values.
#
# ALERT_EMAIL_PROVIDER=smtp
# ALERT_EMAIL_FROM=qodiqa-alerts@<REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_DOMAIN>
# ALERT_EMAIL_TO=soc@<REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_DOMAIN>
#
# --- smtp provider ---
# ALERT_EMAIL_SMTP_HOST=<REPLACE_WITH_SMTP_HOSTNAME>
# ALERT_EMAIL_SMTP_PORT=587
# ALERT_EMAIL_SMTP_USER=<REPLACE_WITH_SMTP_USERNAME>
# ALERT_EMAIL_SMTP_PASS=<REPLACE_WITH_SMTP_PASSWORD>
#
# STARTTLS is negotiated automatically whenever the relay advertises it in
# its EHLO response, and AUTH LOGIN is refused outright if the relay does
# NOT advertise STARTTLS — this prevents credentials ever being sent in
# plaintext. Only set the two flags below if you understand the tradeoff;
# see docs/operations/notification-setup.md §2a for the full explanation.
#
# 'false' to accept a self-signed/internal-CA relay certificate during
# STARTTLS (default: true — verify like any other outbound TLS connection).
# ALERT_EMAIL_SMTP_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=true
#
# 'true' to permit AUTH LOGIN over plaintext when the relay does not
# advertise STARTTLS. Only for a relay reachable exclusively over a trusted
# internal network with no public route (default: unset — refuse).
# ALERT_EMAIL_TRUSTED_RELAY_ONLY=false
#
# --- sendgrid provider (ALERT_EMAIL_PROVIDER=sendgrid) ---
# ALERT_EMAIL_SENDGRID_API_KEY=<REPLACE_WITH_REAL_SENDGRID_KEY>
#
# --- mailgun provider (ALERT_EMAIL_PROVIDER=mailgun) ---
# ALERT_EMAIL_MAILGUN_API_KEY=<REPLACE_WITH_REAL_MAILGUN_KEY>
# ALERT_EMAIL_MAILGUN_DOMAIN=<REPLACE_WITH_MAILGUN_DOMAIN>
#
# ALERT_EMAIL_ENABLED=false

# ── PagerDuty ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    PagerDuty Events API v2 — pages on-call for HIGH/CRITICAL alerts.
# Required:   NO (default: disabled)
# Format:     32-char integration/routing key from a PagerDuty service's
#             "Events API v2" integration.
# Setup:      PagerDuty service -> Integrations -> Add Integration -> Events API v2
#
# ALERT_PAGERDUTY_ROUTING_KEY=<REPLACE_WITH_REAL_32_CHAR_ROUTING_KEY>
# ALERT_PAGERDUTY_URL=https://events.pagerduty.com/v2/enqueue
# ALERT_PAGERDUTY_ENABLED=false

# ── SMS (Twilio, or generic SMS webhook) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    SMS delivery for CRITICAL alerts only (see SeverityModel.js).
# Required:   NO (default: disabled)
# Format:     ALERT_SMS_PROVIDER selects the transport: twilio (default) |
#             webhook (generic SMS gateway HTTP API).
# Security:   Twilio auth token is a secret — never commit real values.
#
# ALERT_SMS_PROVIDER=twilio
# ALERT_SMS_FROM=+1<REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_TWILIO_NUMBER>
# ALERT_SMS_TO=+1<REPLACE_WITH_ONCALL_NUMBER>
# ALERT_SMS_TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=<REPLACE_WITH_REAL_TWILIO_SID>
# ALERT_SMS_TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=<REPLACE_WITH_REAL_TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN>
#
# --- webhook provider (ALERT_SMS_PROVIDER=webhook) ---
# ALERT_SMS_WEBHOOK_URL=<REPLACE_WITH_SMS_GATEWAY_URL>
#
# ALERT_SMS_ENABLED=false

# ── SIEM (Splunk HEC / generic HTTP, or Syslog) ───────────────────────────────
# Purpose:    Forwards alerts in CEF format to a SIEM (Splunk, QRadar, etc.),
#             independent of the full log-shipping pipeline in
#             src/observability/logShipping/ (that ships the entire log
#             stream; this ships alert-derived events only).
# Required:   NO (default: disabled)
# Format:     ALERT_SIEM_MODE selects the transport: http (default, Splunk
#             HTTP Event Collector) | syslog (UDP/TCP).
#
# ALERT_SIEM_MODE=http
# ALERT_SIEM_URL=https://<REPLACE_WITH_SIEM_HEC_HOSTNAME>:8088/services/collector
# ALERT_SIEM_TOKEN=<REPLACE_WITH_REAL_HEC_TOKEN>
#
# --- syslog mode (ALERT_SIEM_MODE=syslog) ---
# ALERT_SIEM_SYSLOG_HOST=<REPLACE_WITH_SYSLOG_RECEIVER_HOST>
# ALERT_SIEM_SYSLOG_PORT=514
# ALERT_SIEM_SYSLOG_PROTO=udp
#
# ALERT_SIEM_SOURCE=qodiqa
# ALERT_SIEM_SOURCETYPE=qodiqa:alert
# ALERT_SIEM_ENABLED=false

# ── Best-effort delivery for the pre-alerting-framework HA startup guard ─────
# Purpose:    Timeout for src/runtime/earlyCriticalNotify.js's internal
#             request lifetime. This is NOT awaited before the guard exits
#             (see src/core/audit/multiInstanceGuard.js) — it only bounds
#             how long the outbound socket is kept open before being torn
#             down, so a hung endpoint can't leak a dangling connection.
# Required:   NO (default: 3000)
# Uses:       ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL (see above) as its destination.
#
QODIQA_CRITICAL_NOTIFY_TIMEOUT_MS=3000

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TENANT SELF-SERVICE PORTAL
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Purpose:    A read-only, RBAC-gated portal (identity/keys/consent) for a
#             tenant's own staff, authenticated via OIDC SSO — mounted at
#             /tenant-portal. and
# for the full design.
# Required:   NO (default: disabled). Every variable below is inert while
#             QODIQA_PORTAL_ENABLED is unset/false.
#
# QODIQA_PORTAL_ENABLED=true
#
# QODIQA_OIDC_ISSUER=https://<REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_IDP_ISSUER_URL>
# QODIQA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=<REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_OIDC_CLIENT_ID>
# QODIQA_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=https://<REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_HOSTNAME>/tenant-portal/callback
# QODIQA_OIDC_TENANT_CLAIM=tenant_id
#
# Security:   QODIQA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET and QODIQA_PORTAL_SESSION_SECRET are
#             managed by QODIQA_CONFIG_SECRETS_PROVIDER when set to a non-"env"
#             value (see the section above) — omit both from this file in
#             that mode. QODIQA_PORTAL_SESSION_SECRET signs tenant-portal
#             session tokens (services/portal/portalSessionToken.js); generate
#             with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# QODIQA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=<REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET>
# QODIQA_PORTAL_SESSION_SECRET=<REPLACE_WITH_A_GENERATED_HEX_SECRET>
#
# QODIQA_PORTAL_SESSION_TTL_MS=28800000
# QODIQA_PORTAL_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=60
# QODIQA_PORTAL_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
#
# Security:   QODIQA_PORTAL_DEV_AUTH_ENABLED must NEVER be set in production —
#             boot validation refuses (FATAL BOOT_VALIDATION_FATAL, variable
#             QODIQA_PORTAL_DEV_AUTH_ENABLED) if NODE_ENV=production and this
#             is "true". Leave unset/commented out here.
#
# QODIQA_PORTAL_DEV_AUTH_ENABLED=false
